{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AsuZdvLzFNH4QSc26uEND1","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# YellowDog DHCP Service Data Persistence - PRD (Updated)\n     2→\n     3→## Overview\n     4→\n     5→**Objective**: Persistent storage layer for DHCPv4/v6 service state, enabling lease management, address allocation, ACL enforcement, and custom option delivery across service restarts.\n     6→\n     7→**Scope**: Both `YellowDog.Dhcpv4` and `YellowDog.Dhcpv6` applications within the umbrella architecture.\n     8→\n     9→---\n    10→\n    11→## Problem Statement\n    12→\n    13→DHCP services require persistent state to:\n    14→- Maintain lease consistency across restarts\n    15→- Enforce address allocation policies\n    16→- Apply client-specific ACLs and options\n    17→- Support network topology requirements (subnets, ranges, reservations)\n    18→\n    19→Current in-memory-only operation loses all state on service restart, breaking RFC compliance and operational requirements.\n    20→\n    21→---\n    22→\n    23→## Data Model\n    24→\n    25→### Address Pools\n    26→\n    27→**DHCPv4 Pool**:\n    28→- Subnet CIDR (network/prefix)\n    29→- Available range(s) within subnet\n    30→- Gateway address\n    31→- DNS servers (list)\n    32→- Domain name\n    33→- Lease duration (default, min, max)\n    34→- Static reservations (MAC → IP mapping)\n    35→\n    36→**DHCPv6 Pool**:\n    37→- Prefix delegation support (IA_PD)\n    38→- Address assignment ranges (IA_NA)\n    39→- Temporary addresses (IA_TA)\n    40→- DNS servers (recursive, search list)\n    41→- Lease lifetimes (preferred, valid)\n    42→- Static assignments (DUID → address/prefix)\n    43→\n    44→**Common**:\n    45→- Pool identifier (unique name)\n    46→- Priority/weight (for pool selection)\n    47→- Enabled/disabled state\n    48→- Metrics (utilization, lease count)\n    49→- Max leases per pool (default: 1000)\n    50→\n    51→### Leases\n    52→\n    53→**Active Lease Record**:\n    54→- Client identifier (MAC/DUID)\n    55→- Assigned address/prefix\n    56→- Pool reference\n    57→- Lease start time\n    58→- Lease expiration time\n    59→- Renewal time (T1)\n    60→- Rebind time (T2)\n    61→- Client hostname (option 12/39)\n    62→- Vendor class identifier\n    63→- State (offered, bound, expired)\n    64→- Conflict count (for tracking reassignment)\n    65→\n    66→**Lease States**:\n    67→- `offered` - DHCPv4 OFFER sent, awaiting REQUEST\n    68→- `bound` - Active lease\n    69→- `released` - Client released early\n    70→- `expired` - Lease timeout reached\n    71→- `declined` - Address conflict detected, auto-reassigning\n    72→\n    73→**Lease Limits**:\n    74→- Default max per pool: 1000 leases\n    75→- Configurable per-pool override\n    76→- Pool allocation fails when limit reached\n    77→\n    78→### Access Control Lists (ACL)\n    79→\n    80→**MAC-based ACL** (v4):\n    81→- MAC address or pattern (glob/regex)\n    82→- Action: allow, deny, pool_assignment\n    83→- Target pool identifier (if allow)\n    84→- Priority order\n    85→\n    86→**DUID-based ACL** (v6):\n    87→- DUID or pattern\n    88→- Action: allow, deny, pool_assignment\n    89→- Target pool/prefix\n    90→- Priority order\n    91→\n    92→**Option-based ACL**:\n    93→- Match conditions:\n    94→  - Vendor Class Identifier (v4 opt 60, v6 opt 16)\n    95→  - User Class (v4 opt 77, v6 opt 15)\n    96→  - Client Architecture (v4 opt 93, v6 opt 61)\n    97→  - Relay Agent Information (v4 opt 82, v6 opt 37)\n    98→- Action: pool_assignment, custom_options, deny\n    99→- Target configuration\n   100→\n   101→### Custom Options\n   102→\n   103→**Option Definition**:\n   104→- Option code (1-254 for v4, 1-65535 for v6)\n   105→- Option name\n   106→- Data type (ip, string, uint8, uint16, uint32, hex)\n   107→- Value or value template\n   108→\n   109→**Option Sets**:\n   110→- Set identifier\n   111→- List of option definitions\n   112→- Application scope:\n   113→  - Global (all clients)\n   114→  - Per-pool\n   115→  - Per-ACL match\n   116→  - Per-static reservation\n   117→\n   118→**Template Variables** (simple substitution only):\n   119→- `${client_mac}` - Client MAC address (v4)\n   120→- `${client_duid}` - Client DUID (v6)\n   121→- `${client_hostname}` - Requested hostname\n   122→- `${lease_address}` - Assigned IP/prefix\n   123→- `${pool_name}` - Pool identifier\n   124→\n   125→**Standard Option Overrides**:\n   126→- Allow per-pool/per-client override of standard options\n   127→- Examples: NTP servers, TFTP boot file, SIP servers\n   128→\n   129→---\n   130→\n   131→## Storage Architecture\n   132→\n   133→### Backend Selection\n   134→\n   135→Support pluggable backends via behavior:\n   136→- **ETS** (default) - In-memory with periodic snapshots to TOML\n   137→- **DETS** - Disk-based ETS for persistence\n   138→- **Mnesia** - Distributed, transactional (future clustering)\n   139→- **External** - PostgreSQL/SQLite via Ecto (high-scale deployments)\n   140→\n   141→### Data Organization\n   142→\n   143→```\n   144→config/\n   145→  dhcpv4_pools.toml      # Pool definitions\n   146→  dhcpv6_pools.toml\n   147→  dhcp_acls.toml         # ACL rules\n   148→  dhcp_options.toml      # Custom option sets\n   149→\n   150→data/\n   151→  dhcpv4_leases.dets     # Active leases\n   152→  dhcpv6_leases.dets\n   153→  dhcp_metrics.dets      # Counters, statistics\n   154→```\n   155→\n   156→### TOML Schema\n   157→\n   158→**Pool Configuration** (`dhcpv4_pools.toml`):\n   159→```toml\n   160→[[pools]]\n   161→name = \"office_network\"\n   162→enabled = true\n   163→subnet = \"192.168.1.0/24\"\n   164→ranges = [\"192.168.1.100-192.168.1.200\"]\n   165→gateway = \"192.168.1.1\"\n   166→dns_servers = [\"8.8.8.8\", \"8.8.4.4\"]\n   167→domain = \"office.local\"\n   168→lease_time = 86400\n   169→max_leases = 1000  # default\n   170→priority = 100\n   171→\n   172→[[pools.reservations]]\n   173→mac = \"aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff\"\n   174→address = \"192.168.1.50\"\n   175→hostname = \"printer-01\"\n   176→```\n   177→\n   178→**ACL Configuration** (`dhcp_acls.toml`):\n   179→```toml\n   180→[[rules]]\n   181→priority = 10\n   182→type = \"mac\"\n   183→pattern = \"aa:bb:cc:*\"\n   184→action = \"allow\"\n   185→target_pool = \"office_network\"\n   186→\n   187→[[rules]]\n   188→priority = 20\n   189→type = \"vendor_class\"\n   190→pattern = \"MSFT 5.0\"\n   191→action = \"custom_options\"\n   192→option_set = \"windows_clients\"\n   193→```\n   194→\n   195→**Custom Options** (`dhcp_options.toml`):\n   196→```toml\n   197→[[option_sets]]\n   198→name = \"windows_clients\"\n   199→scope = \"acl\"\n   200→\n   201→[[option_sets.options]]\n   202→code = 42  # NTP servers\n   203→type = \"ip_list\"\n   204→value = [\"192.168.1.10\", \"192.168.1.11\"]\n   205→\n   206→[[option_sets.options]]\n   207→code = 66  # TFTP server with template\n   208→type = \"string\"\n   209→value = \"tftp-${pool_name}.local\"\n   210→```\n   211→\n   212→---\n   213→\n   214→## Functional Requirements\n   215→\n   216→### FR1: Pool Management\n   217→\n   218→- **FR1.1**: Load pool definitions from TOML on service start\n   219→- **FR1.2**: Validate pool configurations (CIDR validity, range overlaps)\n   220→- **FR1.3**: Dynamic pool enable/disable without service restart\n   221→- **FR1.4**: Pool utilization monitoring (allocated/available addresses)\n   222→- **FR1.5**: Automatic pool selection based on relay agent subnet or client matching\n   223→- **FR1.6**: Enforce max_leases limit per pool (default 1000)\n   224→\n   225→### FR2: Lease Lifecycle\n   226→\n   227→- **FR2.1**: Persist lease on DISCOVER→OFFER→REQUEST→ACK (v4) or SOLICIT→ADVERTISE→REQUEST→REPLY (v6)\n   228→- **FR2.2**: Lease renewal updates expiration times\n   229→- **FR2.3**: Automatic lease expiration cleanup via GenServer worker\n   230→- **FR2.4**: **Conflict Resolution**: On DECLINE/duplicate detection:\n   231→  - Automatically reassign new address from pool\n   232→  - Log warning with client identifier and conflicted address\n   233→  - Increment conflict counter on lease record\n   234→  - Mark conflicted address as temporarily unavailable (configurable quarantine period)\n   235→- **FR2.5**: Manual lease revocation via management API\n   236→\n   237→### FR3: ACL Enforcement\n   238→\n   239→- **FR3.1**: Evaluate ACL rules in priority order (lowest first)\n   240→- **FR3.2**: First matching rule determines action\n   241→- **FR3.3**: No-match defaults to configurable policy (allow/deny)\n   242→- **FR3.4**: ACL-based pool assignment overrides default selection\n   243→- **FR3.5**: ACL rule hot-reload without service interruption\n   244→\n   245→### FR4: Custom Options\n   246→\n   247→- **FR4.1**: Apply option sets based on scope hierarchy:\n   248→  1. Static reservation options\n   249→  2. ACL-matched option sets\n   250→  3. Pool-level options\n   251→  4. Global options\n   252→- **FR4.2**: Option merging strategy (override vs. append for multi-value options)\n   253→- **FR4.3**: **Simple template substitution**:\n   254→  - Variable format: `${variable_name}`\n   255→  - Supported variables: `client_mac`, `client_duid`, `client_hostname`, `lease_address`, `pool_name`\n   256→  - No expression evaluation, no conditionals\n   257→  - Missing variables replaced with empty string\n   258→- **FR4.4**: Validation of option data types before encoding\n   259→\n   260→### FR5: Data Persistence\n   261→\n   262→- **FR5.1**: Atomic lease writes (no partial state)\n   263→- **FR5.2**: Crash recovery restores last consistent state\n   264→- **FR5.3**: Configurable snapshot interval for ETS backend\n   265→- **FR5.4**: Lease database compaction (remove expired entries)\n   266→- **FR5.5**: Export/import for YellowDog TOML format only (no ISC DHCP compatibility)\n   267→\n   268→---\n   269→\n   270→## Non-Functional Requirements\n   271→\n   272→### NFR1: Performance\n   273→\n   274→- Lease allocation: <1ms (ETS/DETS), <5ms (external DB)\n   275→- Support 1000 active leases per pool (configurable up to 10,000)\n   276→- ACL evaluation: <100μs for typical rule sets (<100 rules)\n   277→- Conflict reassignment: <10ms total (detection + new allocation)\n   278→\n   279→### NFR2: Reliability\n   280→\n   281→- No lease state loss on clean shutdown\n   282→- Automatic recovery from corrupted DETS files (fallback to last snapshot)\n   283→- Lease expiration worker must not block service operations\n   284→- Conflict reassignment failures trigger alert but don't block service\n   285→\n   286→### NFR3: Observability\n   287→\n   288→- Telemetry events for:\n   289→  - Lease allocations, renewals, releases\n   290→  - Pool utilization changes\n   291→  - ACL match/deny events\n   292→  - Backend write latency\n   293→  - **Conflict detection and reassignment**\n   294→  - **Pool limit exceeded events**\n   295→- Warning logs for:\n   296→  - Address conflicts with client identifier and reassigned address\n   297→  - Pool approaching capacity (>90%)\n   298→  - Repeated conflicts from same client (>3 per hour)\n   299→\n   300→### NFR4: Configuration\n   301→\n   302→- TOML schema validation on load\n   303→- Clear error messages for misconfiguration\n   304→- Support for configuration file includes (modular organization)\n   305→- Live reload for ACL/option changes (pools require restart)\n   306→\n   307→---\n   308→\n   309→## Integration Points\n   310→\n   311→### With YellowDog.Telemetry\n   312→\n   313→- Emit structured events for lease lifecycle\n   314→- Track pool utilization metrics\n   315→- Record ACL evaluation statistics\n   316→- Alert on conflict patterns\n   317→\n   318→### With ex_dhcp\n   319→\n   320→- Persistence layer provides callbacks for:\n   321→  - Address availability check\n   322→  - Lease creation/renewal\n   323→  - Option generation based on client context\n   324→- Receive parsed request packets, return option lists\n   325→- Handle DECLINE messages for conflict resolution\n   326→\n   327→### With Core YellowDog Application\n   328→\n   329→- Configuration loading from centralized TOML\n   330→- Supervised GenServers for lease management\n   331→- Management API endpoints (future Phoenix dashboard)\n   332→\n   333→---\n   334→\n   335→## Success Metrics\n   336→\n   337→- **Lease Consistency**: 0% lease loss across service restarts\n   338→- **ACL Performance**: 99.9th percentile evaluation time <500μs\n   339→- **Pool Utilization**: Accurate reporting within 1% margin\n   340→- **Configuration Errors**: 100% caught at validation time, not runtime\n   341→- **Conflict Resolution**: Automatic reassignment in <10ms, 0% service disruption\n   342→- **Pool Limits**: Hard enforcement, no overallocation\n   343→\n   344→---\n   345→\n   346→## Design Decisions\n   347→\n   348→### 1. Lease Database Size Limits\n   349→**Decision**: Default max 1000 leases per pool, configurable per-pool override  \n   350→**Rationale**: Targets small-to-medium deployments, prevents runaway allocation\n   351→\n   352→### 2. Conflict Resolution Strategy\n   353→**Decision**: Automatic reassignment with warning logs  \n   354→**Rationale**: Maintains service availability, provides audit trail for investigation\n   355→\n   356→### 3. Template Variable Complexity\n   357→**Decision**: Simple `${var}` substitution only  \n   358→**Rationale**: Covers 95% of use cases, avoids security/complexity of expression languages\n   359→\n   360→### 4. Configuration Migration\n   361→**Decision**: YellowDog TOML format only, no ISC DHCP import  \n   362→**Rationale**: Clean-slate design, users migrate via manual translation or scripts\n   363→\n   364→---\n   365→\n   366→## Future Considerations\n   367→\n   368→- **Clustering**: Mnesia backend for multi-node deployments\n   369→- **Lease History**: Audit trail for compliance requirements\n   370→- **IPAM Integration**: External IP address management system sync\n   371→- **Prefix Delegation**: Advanced DHCPv6 IA_PD with route injection\n   372→- **Dynamic DNS Updates**: RFC 4704 (v6) and RFC 4702 (v4) support\n   373→- **Conflict Quarantine**: Configurable hold-down timer for conflicted addresses\n   374→\n   375→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8559038-3d53-47df-9b7a-d16bbb068c80","uuid":"f55975e9-fcfe-42ef-aab2-73c29b0e6aec","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/prd.md","content":"# YellowDog DHCP Service Data Persistence - PRD (Updated)\n\n## Overview\n\n**Objective**: Persistent storage layer for DHCPv4/v6 service state, enabling lease management, address allocation, ACL enforcement, and custom option delivery across service restarts.\n\n**Scope**: Both `YellowDog.Dhcpv4` and `YellowDog.Dhcpv6` applications within the umbrella architecture.\n\n---\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nDHCP services require persistent state to:\n- Maintain lease consistency across restarts\n- Enforce address allocation policies\n- Apply client-specific ACLs and options\n- Support network topology requirements (subnets, ranges, reservations)\n\nCurrent in-memory-only operation loses all state on service restart, breaking RFC compliance and operational requirements.\n\n---\n\n## Data Model\n\n### Address Pools\n\n**DHCPv4 Pool**:\n- Subnet CIDR (network/prefix)\n- Available range(s) within subnet\n- Gateway address\n- DNS servers (list)\n- Domain name\n- Lease duration (default, min, max)\n- Static reservations (MAC → IP mapping)\n\n**DHCPv6 Pool**:\n- Prefix delegation support (IA_PD)\n- Address assignment ranges (IA_NA)\n- Temporary addresses (IA_TA)\n- DNS servers (recursive, search list)\n- Lease lifetimes (preferred, valid)\n- Static assignments (DUID → address/prefix)\n\n**Common**:\n- Pool identifier (unique name)\n- Priority/weight (for pool selection)\n- Enabled/disabled state\n- Metrics (utilization, lease count)\n- Max leases per pool (default: 1000)\n\n### Leases\n\n**Active Lease Record**:\n- Client identifier (MAC/DUID)\n- Assigned address/prefix\n- Pool reference\n- Lease start time\n- Lease expiration time\n- Renewal time (T1)\n- Rebind time (T2)\n- Client hostname (option 12/39)\n- Vendor class identifier\n- State (offered, bound, expired)\n- Conflict count (for tracking reassignment)\n\n**Lease States**:\n- `offered` - DHCPv4 OFFER sent, awaiting REQUEST\n- `bound` - Active lease\n- `released` - Client released early\n- `expired` - Lease timeout reached\n- `declined` - Address conflict detected, auto-reassigning\n\n**Lease Limits**:\n- Default max per pool: 1000 leases\n- Configurable per-pool override\n- Pool allocation fails when limit reached\n\n### Access Control Lists (ACL)\n\n**MAC-based ACL** (v4):\n- MAC address or pattern (glob/regex)\n- Action: allow, deny, pool_assignment\n- Target pool identifier (if allow)\n- Priority order\n\n**DUID-based ACL** (v6):\n- DUID or pattern\n- Action: allow, deny, pool_assignment\n- Target pool/prefix\n- Priority order\n\n**Option-based ACL**:\n- Match conditions:\n  - Vendor Class Identifier (v4 opt 60, v6 opt 16)\n  - User Class (v4 opt 77, v6 opt 15)\n  - Client Architecture (v4 opt 93, v6 opt 61)\n  - Relay Agent Information (v4 opt 82, v6 opt 37)\n- Action: pool_assignment, custom_options, deny\n- Target configuration\n\n### Custom Options\n\n**Option Definition**:\n- Option code (1-254 for v4, 1-65535 for v6)\n- Option name\n- Data type (ip, string, uint8, uint16, uint32, hex)\n- Value or value template\n\n**Option Sets**:\n- Set identifier\n- List of option definitions\n- Application scope:\n  - Global (all clients)\n  - Per-pool\n  - Per-ACL match\n  - Per-static reservation\n\n**Template Variables** (simple substitution only):\n- `${client_mac}` - Client MAC address (v4)\n- `${client_duid}` - Client DUID (v6)\n- `${client_hostname}` - Requested hostname\n- `${lease_address}` - Assigned IP/prefix\n- `${pool_name}` - Pool identifier\n\n**Standard Option Overrides**:\n- Allow per-pool/per-client override of standard options\n- Examples: NTP servers, TFTP boot file, SIP servers\n\n---\n\n## Storage Architecture\n\n### Backend Selection\n\nSupport pluggable backends via behavior:\n- **ETS** (default) - In-memory with periodic snapshots to TOML\n- **DETS** - Disk-based ETS for persistence\n- **Mnesia** - Distributed, transactional (future clustering)\n- **External** - PostgreSQL/SQLite via Ecto (high-scale deployments)\n\n### Data Organization\n\n```\nconfig/\n  dhcpv4_pools.toml      # Pool definitions\n  dhcpv6_pools.toml\n  dhcp_acls.toml         # ACL rules\n  dhcp_options.toml      # Custom option sets\n\ndata/\n  dhcpv4_leases.dets     # Active leases\n  dhcpv6_leases.dets\n  dhcp_metrics.dets      # Counters, statistics\n```\n\n### TOML Schema\n\n**Pool Configuration** (`dhcpv4_pools.toml`):\n```toml\n[[pools]]\nname = \"office_network\"\nenabled = true\nsubnet = \"192.168.1.0/24\"\nranges = [\"192.168.1.100-192.168.1.200\"]\ngateway = \"192.168.1.1\"\ndns_servers = [\"8.8.8.8\", \"8.8.4.4\"]\ndomain = \"office.local\"\nlease_time = 86400\nmax_leases = 1000  # default\npriority = 100\n\n[[pools.reservations]]\nmac = \"aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff\"\naddress = \"192.168.1.50\"\nhostname = \"printer-01\"\n```\n\n**ACL Configuration** (`dhcp_acls.toml`):\n```toml\n[[rules]]\npriority = 10\ntype = \"mac\"\npattern = \"aa:bb:cc:*\"\naction = \"allow\"\ntarget_pool = \"office_network\"\n\n[[rules]]\npriority = 20\ntype = \"vendor_class\"\npattern = \"MSFT 5.0\"\naction = \"custom_options\"\noption_set = \"windows_clients\"\n```\n\n**Custom Options** (`dhcp_options.toml`):\n```toml\n[[option_sets]]\nname = \"windows_clients\"\nscope = \"acl\"\n\n[[option_sets.options]]\ncode = 42  # NTP servers\ntype = \"ip_list\"\nvalue = [\"192.168.1.10\", \"192.168.1.11\"]\n\n[[option_sets.options]]\ncode = 66  # TFTP server with template\ntype = \"string\"\nvalue = \"tftp-${pool_name}.local\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n### FR1: Pool Management\n\n- **FR1.1**: Load pool definitions from TOML on service start\n- **FR1.2**: Validate pool configurations (CIDR validity, range overlaps)\n- **FR1.3**: Dynamic pool enable/disable without service restart\n- **FR1.4**: Pool utilization monitoring (allocated/available addresses)\n- **FR1.5**: Automatic pool selection based on relay agent subnet or client matching\n- **FR1.6**: Enforce max_leases limit per pool (default 1000)\n\n### FR2: Lease Lifecycle\n\n- **FR2.1**: Persist lease on DISCOVER→OFFER→REQUEST→ACK (v4) or SOLICIT→ADVERTISE→REQUEST→REPLY (v6)\n- **FR2.2**: Lease renewal updates expiration times\n- **FR2.3**: Automatic lease expiration cleanup via GenServer worker\n- **FR2.4**: **Conflict Resolution**: On DECLINE/duplicate detection:\n  - Automatically reassign new address from pool\n  - Log warning with client identifier and conflicted address\n  - Increment conflict counter on lease record\n  - Mark conflicted address as temporarily unavailable (configurable quarantine period)\n- **FR2.5**: Manual lease revocation via management API\n\n### FR3: ACL Enforcement\n\n- **FR3.1**: Evaluate ACL rules in priority order (lowest first)\n- **FR3.2**: First matching rule determines action\n- **FR3.3**: No-match defaults to configurable policy (allow/deny)\n- **FR3.4**: ACL-based pool assignment overrides default selection\n- **FR3.5**: ACL rule hot-reload without service interruption\n\n### FR4: Custom Options\n\n- **FR4.1**: Apply option sets based on scope hierarchy:\n  1. Static reservation options\n  2. ACL-matched option sets\n  3. Pool-level options\n  4. Global options\n- **FR4.2**: Option merging strategy (override vs. append for multi-value options)\n- **FR4.3**: **Simple template substitution**:\n  - Variable format: `${variable_name}`\n  - Supported variables: `client_mac`, `client_duid`, `client_hostname`, `lease_address`, `pool_name`\n  - No expression evaluation, no conditionals\n  - Missing variables replaced with empty string\n- **FR4.4**: Validation of option data types before encoding\n\n### FR5: Data Persistence\n\n- **FR5.1**: Atomic lease writes (no partial state)\n- **FR5.2**: Crash recovery restores last consistent state\n- **FR5.3**: Configurable snapshot interval for ETS backend\n- **FR5.4**: Lease database compaction (remove expired entries)\n- **FR5.5**: Export/import for YellowDog TOML format only (no ISC DHCP compatibility)\n\n---\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### NFR1: Performance\n\n- Lease allocation: <1ms (ETS/DETS), <5ms (external DB)\n- Support 1000 active leases per pool (configurable up to 10,000)\n- ACL evaluation: <100μs for typical rule sets (<100 rules)\n- Conflict reassignment: <10ms total (detection + new allocation)\n\n### NFR2: Reliability\n\n- No lease state loss on clean shutdown\n- Automatic recovery from corrupted DETS files (fallback to last snapshot)\n- Lease expiration worker must not block service operations\n- Conflict reassignment failures trigger alert but don't block service\n\n### NFR3: Observability\n\n- Telemetry events for:\n  - Lease allocations, renewals, releases\n  - Pool utilization changes\n  - ACL match/deny events\n  - Backend write latency\n  - **Conflict detection and reassignment**\n  - **Pool limit exceeded events**\n- Warning logs for:\n  - Address conflicts with client identifier and reassigned address\n  - Pool approaching capacity (>90%)\n  - Repeated conflicts from same client (>3 per hour)\n\n### NFR4: Configuration\n\n- TOML schema validation on load\n- Clear error messages for misconfiguration\n- Support for configuration file includes (modular organization)\n- Live reload for ACL/option changes (pools require restart)\n\n---\n\n## Integration Points\n\n### With YellowDog.Telemetry\n\n- Emit structured events for lease lifecycle\n- Track pool utilization metrics\n- Record ACL evaluation statistics\n- Alert on conflict patterns\n\n### With ex_dhcp\n\n- Persistence layer provides callbacks for:\n  - Address availability check\n  - Lease creation/renewal\n  - Option generation based on client context\n- Receive parsed request packets, return option lists\n- Handle DECLINE messages for conflict resolution\n\n### With Core YellowDog Application\n\n- Configuration loading from centralized TOML\n- Supervised GenServers for lease management\n- Management API endpoints (future Phoenix dashboard)\n\n---\n\n## Success Metrics\n\n- **Lease Consistency**: 0% lease loss across service restarts\n- **ACL Performance**: 99.9th percentile evaluation time <500μs\n- **Pool Utilization**: Accurate reporting within 1% margin\n- **Configuration Errors**: 100% caught at validation time, not runtime\n- **Conflict Resolution**: Automatic reassignment in <10ms, 0% service disruption\n- **Pool Limits**: Hard enforcement, no overallocation\n\n---\n\n## Design Decisions\n\n### 1. Lease Database Size Limits\n**Decision**: Default max 1000 leases per pool, configurable per-pool override  \n**Rationale**: Targets small-to-medium deployments, prevents runaway allocation\n\n### 2. Conflict Resolution Strategy\n**Decision**: Automatic reassignment with warning logs  \n**Rationale**: Maintains service availability, provides audit trail for investigation\n\n### 3. Template Variable Complexity\n**Decision**: Simple `${var}` substitution only  \n**Rationale**: Covers 95% of use cases, avoids security/complexity of expression languages\n\n### 4. Configuration Migration\n**Decision**: YellowDog TOML format only, no ISC DHCP import  \n**Rationale**: Clean-slate design, users migrate via manual translation or scripts\n\n---\n\n## Future Considerations\n\n- **Clustering**: Mnesia backend for multi-node deployments\n- **Lease History**: Audit trail for compliance requirements\n- **IPAM Integration**: External IP address management system sync\n- **Prefix Delegation**: Advanced DHCPv6 IA_PD with route injection\n- **Dynamic DNS Updates**: RFC 4704 (v6) and RFC 4702 (v4) support\n- **Conflict Quarantine**: Configurable hold-down timer for conflicted addresses\n\n","numLines":375,"startLine":1,"totalLines":375}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_016xcmzoB5v4a72QGQd3fRtm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## REASON Phase\n\n**Current State Analysis:**\n- **Phase**: CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT, Iteration 2\n- **In-Progress**: Performance optimization review\n- **Resources**: CPU 16%, Memory 75% - OK to proceed\n- **PRD Goal**: DHCP service data persistence with TOML-based configuration, pool management, ACL enforcement, and conflict resolution\n\n**Learnings to Apply:**\n1. Handler modules need `use Abyss.Handler` \n2. Blocking recv prevents GenServer responsiveness\n3. Silent fallbacks mask bugs - use logging\n4. RFC compliance matters for protocol validation\n\n**PRD Analysis - Key Requirements:**\n1. **Pool Management** - Load from TOML, validation, pool limits (max 1000 leases default)\n2. **Lease Lifecycle** - Persist leases, conflict resolution with auto-reassignment\n3. **ACL Enforcement** - Priority-ordered rules, MAC/DUID/option-based matching\n4. **Custom Options** - Template variables, scope hierarchy\n5. **Storage** - ETS default with DETS persistence, atomic writes\n\n**Current Implementation Gap:**\nThe existing `YellowDog.Dhcpv4.LeaseManager` and `YellowDog.Dhcpv6.LeaseManager` use ETS for in-memory storage but lack:\n- TOML pool configuration loading\n- ACL rules enforcement\n- Max lease limits per pool\n- Conflict detection/reassignment\n- DETS persistence\n\nLet me check the current implementation to understand what exists."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7131,"cache_read_input_tokens":54509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7131,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8559038-3d53-47df-9b7a-d16bbb068c80","uuid":"a1452f3d-ec8d-4410-a929-54b6af091f0c"}
